## 📂 Project Structure

```
server/
├── .cursorrules           # This file (index of rules)
├── README.md             # Project README
│
├── rules/                # Modular rule files
│   ├── README.md
│   ├── 01-database.md
│   ├── 02-architecture.md
│   ├── 03-service-layer.md
│   ├── 04-repository-layer.md
│   ├── 05-api-development.md
│   ├── 06-documentation.md
│   └── 07-doc-organization.md
│
├── docs/                 # Documentation
│   ├── YYYY-MM-DD/      # Date-based docs
│   ├── features/        # Feature specs
│   └── [TRDs & journeys]
│
└── app/                  # Application code
    ├── models/          # Database models
    ├── repositories/    # Data access layer
    ├── services/        # Business logic layer
    ├── controllers/     # HTTP layer
    └── routes/          # API endpoints
```

---

## 🎯 For Cursor AI

When generating or modifying code:
1. ✅ Follow clean architecture (no layer bypassing)
2. ✅ Never inject `Session` into services
3. ✅ Never create database ENUMs
4. ✅ Put all docs in `docs/YYYY-MM-DD/` folders
5. ✅ Enforce RBAC at route level
6. ✅ Document all functions with docstrings
7. ✅ Use descriptive UPPERCASE names for docs

---

## 🔄 Rule Updates

To update rules:
1. Edit the relevant file in `rules/`
2. Update examples if needed
3. Keep this index in sync
4. Document rationale for changes

---

## 📝 Documentation Standards

- Every function needs docstring with Args, Returns, Raises
- Pydantic schemas need examples
- API routes need full documentation
- Complex logic needs inline comments
- All implementation docs go in `docs/YYYY-MM-DD/`

---

## ✅ Compliance

These rules are **mandatory** for all code in this project.

Cursor AI automatically enforces these rules when generating code.

For detailed information on any rule, see the corresponding file in the `rules/` directory.

